panvk/kmod: the job manager's waits for idle and for the device's end have a time limit

In the tree the waits of a JM queue have no limit: vkQueueWaitIdle (and so vkDeviceWaitIdle, which
apps call at their end) and destroy_gpu_queue wait for every open submission
(pan_kmod_kbase_wait_seq), and kbase_queue_finish joins the event thread, which ends only when no
submission is in flight (a fence atom's too). An atom that never reports keeps each of them
waiting for ever, and so the process from ending.

This patch (PAN_KMOD_KBASE_END_*_TIMEOUT_NS):
- vkQueueWaitIdle waits up to 10 s (longer than kbase's default job time-out: a hard stop after 50
  and a reset after 55 scheduling ticks of 100 ms) and then marks the queue lost:
  VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST.
- destroy_gpu_queue waits up to 10 s (not at all on a lost queue). When work is still open then, it
  logs so and calls pan_kmod_kbase_abandon(): the submissions in flight run no more callback (it
  would use the queue and the command buffers that go now), and it logs the queue's state (the
  submissions still open, the atoms in flight, the callbacks running) and each atom still in flight
  ("kbase: atom N (submission S) has not reported"); the event thread takes each callback under
  the queue's lock, and abandon waits until none runs.
- After kbase_queue_finish the event thread waits 2 s for the atoms still in flight, logs each one
  ("kbase: atom N (submission S) never reported") and ends without them.
- pan_kmod_kbase_wait_seq_timeout takes 0 for "everything submitted so far", as
  pan_kmod_kbase_wait_seq does (the tree has no caller of it).
Patch 0009 changes how the abandoned work ends, which waits have the limit, and from when it
counts.

